A||||9||Cheever2||Deep water||||4||
Z||000000||Cheever2||06-09-2001||03:37 AM||||You wouldn't believe how deep the water was that I drove my 88 GT through after work tonight to get home.  I am so shocked to discover that these cars can do that.  Tropical Storm Allison came to Houston the other day and stalled out right on top of us, and hasn't moved.  It just keeps pulling up moisture from the Gulf and dumping it on us.  Where I work on 2nd shift, there was 15 inches of rainfall just while I was there tonight.  When I left my building I had no idea how bad it was, but when you get into deep water you can't just turn around.  At one point I had water coming up over the hood onto the bottom of the windshield.  But if I had stayed at work my car might already be under water.  I am so fortunate.  My home is one of the few that has electricity AND is dry inside.  They cancelled tomorrow's car show, but that is now the last thing on my mind.  We just want to see this rain move on out of here!  But now you know that you CAN drive through extremely deep water if you have to.  I just don't recommend it.  I'll have to assess the damage tomorrow in the daylight.<P><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||RossT||06-09-2001||04:21 AM||||If you got water inside, make sure you take your seats out to dry the carpet underneath.<BR>At last weeks Dells Run, I found that my t-tops leaked.  After 3 days of rain, the carpet was dry in the area of the floor mats, but it had flowed to the lower point under the seats!! After 5 boxes of tissue and<BR>4 rolls of toilet paper (thank you host hotel) to blot up most of it, I took the seat out and placed a small space heater to dry up the rest. My t-top leak was a easy fix!! The car had never been rained on, so I nevery knew they leaked.<P>Just stick you hand under the seats to see if its wet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||06-09-2001||05:57 PM||||Aw Cheever; you didn't have to share that rainy news! I'm headed that way next weekend<BR>and all that rain means a muddy mess and a LOT more mosquitos. Keep the insects but send the rain to San Angelo, we're dry as a bone.||||reg||11
Z||000003||Alex4mula||06-09-2001||08:10 PM||||I know what you mean. I once went into a big puddle and just in the midle of it I realy found out how deep it was. Water started to get over the hood and I got scared that the car staled. It went thru without hesitation. No damage anywhere but scary...||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||06-09-2001||11:14 PM||||I wondered if this subject was ever going to come up.  It's true - the Fieros really seem to have a knack for treading water!<P>Years ago in Wichita, driving to work at about 5am, I found myself driving into increasingly deep water.  After thinking for about the 3rd time, "The water will <B>surely</B> get shallower if I keep going," and watching the tide over the front hood, I had had enough.  Even my wallet was getting wet.<P>I stopped (belts squealing, all the time fearing that the engine might stall), backed into a side street, and turned back towards home, towards the shallower water.  Pulled up into a corner gas station, opened the drivers side door, and water actually ran <B>out</B> of the car.<P>The sun came out later, but what a miserable day that was!  Went home, called into work, took the seats out.  Took them into the basement to dry.  Put a fan in the car to dry it out.  Still the interior smelled like a lake.  Finally got the smell out using that powdered sprinkle-on and vacuum-off carpet cleaner/deodorizer.   ||||reg||6
Z||000005||ray b||06-10-2001||12:33 AM||||WATER THAT DEEP WILL WASH OUT GREASE relube every thing on the car that may be dry now before it fails a can of grease is very cheap<BR>fix if used in time.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||06-10-2001||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>WATER THAT DEEP WILL WASH OUT GREASE relube every thing on the car that may be dry now before it fails a can of grease is very cheap<BR>fix if used in time.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Apsitivly, when I worked on Chinook helecopters we had to repack the wheel bearings after every water landing. Man what a PITA that was. Anywho cars are designed to take water much better than a helecopter but just to be sure I would regrease and repack everything.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000007||theogre||06-10-2001||12:55 PM||||Checking grease on everything is a really good idea...<P>Water that deep... I'd check the transmission and engine oil. The engine is probably ok, but the tranny doesn't get hot enough to cook out any water that got in there.<P>If you can get the brake bleeders open, it wouldn't hurt to flood some fresh fluid into the system. (It probably needs it anyway.) I'd use Valvoline synthetic because of it's higher boiling temps. Suck out the fluid in the MC tank before you start. The more old fluid you get out of the system the better.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cheever2||06-10-2001||02:12 PM||||Thanks a lot, guys.  In times like these you don't think clearly, and when you do, it's about the more important things in life.  We've got 9 hospitals closed, thousands of people in shelters, and more waiting on their rooftops (some for days) to be rescued.  This tropical storm brought more water than any hurricane!<P>Wouldn't you know it?  I think the skies are finally safe enough to go open the doors, take out the seats & put em on the driveway, then get some towels to get the standing water out... and as soon as I do, it starts raining again!  I had to run to get the seats into the garage.  (Can't put the GT itself in the garage cuz the Coupe which I got ready for the <B>canceled</B> car show is in there, and I'm <I>not</I> going to pull it out into this weather, after all that work)<P>I'm never selling the Expeditor.  Me and that little car have been through a lot together.  Funny how it always has something to do with heavy rain.  That's what caused the wreck which took it off the road for so long.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000009||87GT||06-10-2001||05:15 PM||||The meteorologist in Tampa say it would take a storm like Allison to get our water back to normal!  Were about 32 inches below normal that past 3 years.||||reg||1
